{
  "version": 3,
  "title": "What if you stepped onto Mars?",
  "series": "Field Study / One Human on Mars",
  "narration": "What if you stepped onto Mars dressed exactly like you are right now? First boot in the dust, great view, absolutely terrible place to take a breath. The air is mostly carbon dioxide and the pressure is less than one percent of Earth's at sea level. You don't explode and you don't instantly freeze. You run out of usable oxygen, black out in seconds, and without rescue, you die. So rewind that step. This time give yourself a sealed pressure suit. Now there's enough pressure around your body, but a sealed bubble of air isn't a lifetime supply. Clip on an oxygen tank and a regulator. You can breathe in. Trouble is, every time you breathe out, carbon dioxide stays in the loop. More oxygen won't make that disappear. Run the air through a scrubber and send it back to the helmet. Now walk up that ridge. Even on a cold planet, your muscles make heat, and a sealed suit traps it. Put water tubes against your skin. A pump carries that heat to a unit on the pack that dumps it outside. You've bought yourself a walk, not a home. Before the pack runs down, head for a shelter. Close the outer hatch, bring the pressure up, and only then take off the helmet. Inside, you need drinking water, food, and power to keep that air moving while you sleep. Put solar panels out in the sunlight and charge batteries for the night. For a longer stay, add shielding over the living space. Mars has so little air overhead that much more radiation reaches the ground. That first footprint took one step. Coming back tomorrow to make a second one? That took a whole little piece of Earth.",
  "voiceover": "episodes/mars-voice.wav",
